---
/** The About page of either site: how both questions are answered, the site's own first. */
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import AboutWet from './AboutWet.astro';
import { BLEND, MODELS, SITES, VERDICT, type Topic } from '@isithot/core';

interface Props {
  topic: Topic;
}
const { topic } = Astro.props;
const names = MODELS.map((m) => m.name);
const modelList = `${names.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${names.at(-1)}`;
const agreeExample = `"5 of 6 forecasts agree within ${BLEND.agreeWithinC}°"`;
const site = SITES[topic].name;
const other = SITES[topic === 'hot' ? 'wet' : 'hot'].name;
const intro = {
  hot: 'will today feel hot where you are? Here is how we get from a pile of forecasts to a single Yes, Almost or No.',
  wet: 'will it rain where you are today? Here is how we get from a pile of forecasts to a single Yes, Maybe or No.',
}[topic];
const description = {
  hot: `How ${site} blends several forecast models and judges hot against what is normal for your place and time of year.`,
  wet: `How ${site} blends several forecast models into one answer about rain: how likely it is, how much and when.`,
}[topic];
---

<Content title="How it works" path="/about" description={description} topic={topic}>
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p>
    {site} answers one question: {intro}
  </p>
  <p>
    {site} and {other} are one service with two questions: will today feel hot, and will it rain?
    Each site opens on its own question, and the Hot and Wet switch at the top of the page swaps
    between them.
  </p>

  {topic === 'wet' && <AboutWet />}

  <h2>Several forecasts, one answer</h2>
  <p>
    Weather services run their own computer models, and they rarely agree to the degree. We ask
    {MODELS.length} of them for today's high: {modelList}. Some models do not cover every place, so
    we use whichever ones answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    With five or more forecasts we drop the single highest and lowest, then average the rest. That
    keeps one wild forecast from dragging the answer around. We do the same for the "feels like"
    temperature, which accounts for humidity and wind.
  </p>

  <h2>How much the forecasts agree</h2>
  <p>
    Under the answer you will see something like {agreeExample}. Each dot on the strip is one
    forecast; the shaded band covers {BLEND.agreeWithinC}° either side of the blended high. When
    most dots sit inside the band, you can trust the answer. When they are scattered, the weather is
    genuinely uncertain, and it is worth checking a detailed forecast.
  </p>

  <h2>Hot for where you are</h2>
  <p>
    24°C feels hot in Newcastle and mild in Seville. So we compare today's feels-like high with the
    long-term climate where you are: how warm your coldest and warmest months normally get. Half of
    the verdict comes from that local comparison and half from the temperature itself, because
    people feel both. A few common-sense limits apply: below {VERDICT.guards.notHotBelow}°C it is
    never "hot", and above {VERDICT.guards.notColdAbove}°C it is never "cold".
  </p>
  <p>
    The "warmer than usual" line compares today's high with the normal high for this date. Normals
    come from NASA's long-term records on a grid about 50 km across, so they describe the regional
    average; coasts, hills and city centres can run a degree or two either side.
  </p>

  {topic === 'hot' && <AboutWet />}

  <h2>Your location</h2>
  <p>
    We start with an approximate location from your internet connection. If you allow it, your
    device location is used instead, rounded to about 5 km before it leaves your browser. You can
    also search for any place. Read more in our <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
  <ul>
    <!-- prettier-ignore -->
    <li>Forecasts: <a href="https://open-meteo.com/" rel="noopener">Open-Meteo.com</a>, licensed under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>.</li>
    <!-- prettier-ignore -->
    <li>Climate normals: <a href="https://power.larc.nasa.gov/" rel="noopener">NASA POWER</a> (Prediction Of Worldwide Energy Resources), long-term monthly averages of daily highs and of rainfall.</li>
    <!-- prettier-ignore -->
    <li>Place names: <a href="https://www.geonames.org/" rel="noopener">GeoNames</a>, licensed under CC BY 4.0.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Forecasts are estimates. For anything that matters, like heat health or flood warnings, check
    your national weather service.
  </p>
</Content>
